WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:05.110
Ant-Man and the Wasp gave fans another look
at the size-changing shenanigans of Paul Rudd's

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:10.080
Ant-Man and added in more characters, more
action, and equal billing for Evangeline Lilly's

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.080
Wasp.

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:14.930
As you might expect for a movie about characters
that shrink down to micro-size, there were

00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:17.360
some very small details that you might have
missed.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:21.741
From a reference to an obscure comic character
to a classic sci-fi movie shout out, here

00:00:21.741 --> 00:00:25.430
are even smaller details you may have missed
in Ant-Man and the Wasp.

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:26.930
"Here's the deal.

00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:28.060
Just give me the facts."

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:28.780
"Just the facts.

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:29.520
Only the facts."

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:30.540
"Breathe.

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:31.580
Focus.

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:33.259
Keep it simple."

00:00:33.259 --> 00:00:34.259
"No doubt, no doubt."

00:00:35.259 --> 00:00:36.259
X-Con Security solutions

00:00:36.259 --> 00:00:40.219
The plot of Ant-Man and the Wasp is focused
on saving Janet Van Dyne from the Quantum

00:00:40.219 --> 00:00:43.840
Realm, but we also see what Scott's been doing
since the last film.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:48.540
He's started a small business with Luis and
company: X-Con, a security consulting service.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:53.190
It's a reference straight from 2015's Ant-Man
comic series, in which Scott Lang starts a

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:56.579
security consulting service: "Ant-Man Security
Solutions."

00:00:56.579 --> 00:01:00.780
The biggest difference besides the names is
that the comics version features the Grizzly,

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:05.029
a giant bear-man ex-supervillain, as his business
partner-slash-employee.

00:01:05.029 --> 00:01:08.689
Here's hoping that this particular detail
somehow makes it into the next Ant-Man movie

00:01:08.689 --> 00:01:09.689
as well.

00:01:09.689 --> 00:01:10.689
"I wish I had a suit.

00:01:10.689 --> 00:01:11.689
Even with like minimal powers."

00:01:11.689 --> 00:01:13.499
"Or maybe even just a suit with no powers."

00:01:13.499 --> 00:01:17.969
Who wouldn't want to see Michael Peña suit
up in a giant bear costume on the big screen?

00:01:17.969 --> 00:01:19.429
Eye see what you did there

00:01:19.429 --> 00:01:22.999
Early in the film, Scott builds an obstacle
course for his daughter out of cardboard.

00:01:22.999 --> 00:01:27.140
That bit of father-daughter bonding might
be adorable, but it also hides a reference

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:29.709
to one of Marvel's strangest villains: the
Orb.

00:01:29.709 --> 00:01:33.459
In order to crack the "retinal scanner" that
Scott has assembled, Cassie has to put on

00:01:33.459 --> 00:01:38.079
a giant eyeball mask, which looks suspiciously
similar to the Orb, a Marvel supervillain

00:01:38.079 --> 00:01:39.259
whose head is a giant eyeball.

00:01:39.259 --> 00:01:43.189
While it seems like a bit of a stretch, the
Orb actually became a pretty major player

00:01:43.189 --> 00:01:46.039
in the last decade of Marvel Comics.

00:01:46.039 --> 00:01:51.119
In 2014's Original Sin storyline, he successfully
steals an eye from the cosmic deity, the Watcher,

00:01:51.119 --> 00:01:55.829
forces dozens of heroes to confront long-buried
secrets, and even becomes a pseudo-Watcher

00:01:55.829 --> 00:01:56.829
himself.

00:01:56.829 --> 00:02:00.649
Is Cassie's mask a hint that there's another
enemy for the Avengers to fight after Thanos

00:02:00.649 --> 00:02:01.649
is taken care of?

00:02:01.649 --> 00:02:04.189
We'll have to keep our eyes peeled.

00:02:04.189 --> 00:02:05.189
Magic man

00:02:05.189 --> 00:02:08.740
Scott's struggle to keep Cassie entertained
while he's under house arrest involves more

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:12.879
than just building giant cardboard obstacle
courses: he also becomes an expert at sleight

00:02:12.879 --> 00:02:14.900
of hand thanks to an online magic course.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:19.019
It's no wonder that Scott becomes pretty good
at magic, since his online teacher is actually

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:22.200
played by an expert magician: Blake Vogt.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:23.500
"Watch, ready?"

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:33.460
Vogt has made a name for himself as a magic
consultant on movies like Now You See Me,

00:02:33.469 --> 00:02:36.620
and he contributes his expertise to Ant-Man
and the Wasp as well.

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:41.260
He even cameos as Scott's online teacher,
briefly visible while Scott, and later FBI

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:43.890
agent Jimmy Woo, are learning close-up magic.

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:45.450
You know what this means, don't you?

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:48.990
Now Dr. Strange isn't the only magic user
in the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:53.079
"So, Earth has, uh, wizards now?"

00:02:53.079 --> 00:02:54.319
Really small details

00:02:54.319 --> 00:02:58.450
While size-changing was a big part of the
original Ant-Man, the sequel makes the Pym

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:01.049
Particles an even bigger part of the story.

00:03:01.049 --> 00:03:05.090
Hank Pym has a laboratory that shrinks down
into a piece of luggage, complete with a handle

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:07.280
and wheels for maximum convenience.

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:10.900
The Quantum Tunnel that they construct to
save Janet from the Quantum Realm is a fun

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:15.680
mix of low and high-tech, combining intricate
computer parts with small items enlarged to

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:16.680
match.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:20.080
"I like the idea that Hank Pym is not Tony
Stark.

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:22.040
It's not super sleek high tech.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:25.920
It's sort of glued together, and he uses a
lot of found objects."

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:30.610
Keep your eyes peeled in the movie for giant
volume knobs that help calibrate Janet's location,

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:35.000
giant bread clips holding wires together,
and plenty of other enlarged household objects

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:36.220
used for the Quantum Tunnel.

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:40.359
It might be true that one man's trash is another
man's treasure, but it's probably a bit more

00:03:40.359 --> 00:03:43.510
convenient when that trash is expanded to
giant size.

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:44.510
The Matrix

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:49.629
Laurence Fishburne is an accomplished actor
who's appeared in dozens of movies, and, well,

00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:50.160
this…

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:51.160
"Ooooweeee!"

00:03:51.629 --> 00:03:55.540
...but he's arguably best-known for playing
Morpheus, the mysterious leader of the freedom

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:57.439
fighters in The Matrix.

00:03:57.439 --> 00:04:01.189
Fishburne's Bill Foster doesn't share too
many qualities with the martial arts master

00:04:01.189 --> 00:04:05.420
Morpheus, but Ant-Man and the Wasp still has
a small reference to Fishburne's most famous

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:06.420
role.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:09.930
When Scott, Hank, and Hope go to convince
Bill to help them track down Pym's stolen

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:14.219
technology, Bill is teaching a class on quantum
entanglement, and the blackboard behind him

00:04:14.219 --> 00:04:16.609
is covered with mathematical equations.

00:04:16.609 --> 00:04:20.639
One equation right next to Bill's head in
the shot even includes the word "Matrix."

00:04:20.639 --> 00:04:22.160
As Keanu Reeves' Neo would say:

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:23.160
"Woah."

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:24.610
Thanks for watching!

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:27.220
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:29.520
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

